questions.explanation:
`var` is function-scoped, hoisted and initialised to `undefined`, and can be redeclared. `let` and `const` are block-scoped and live in the temporal dead zone (TDZ) until their declaration runs; touching them earlier throws a `ReferenceError`. `const` forbids reassigning the binding, not mutating the object it points to.

Why the others are wrong: `let`/`const` are hoisted but not initialised, so early access throws instead of giving `undefined`, and `const` does not freeze the object; `var` is function-scoped, not block-scoped; `var` inside a function is not global and `let` is block-scoped, not function-scoped.

```js
console.log(a); // undefined
var a = 1;
// console.log(b); // ReferenceError (TDZ)
let b = 2;
const o = { n: 1 };
o.n = 2;        // fine
// o = {};      // TypeError
```

Declarations are registered when the scope is created, before code runs. Function declarations are hoisted with their body, so you can call them early. `var` is hoisted as `undefined`, so calling a `var` function expression early is calling `undefined`. `let`, `const` and `class` are hoisted but not initialised (TDZ).

Why the others are wrong: `var` is hoisted (as `undefined`), so the early call throws a `TypeError` (`bye is not a function`), not a `ReferenceError`; the TDZ applies to `let`, `const` and `class`, not `var`; and a `var` function expression is not hoisted with its body, the call fails rather than returning `undefined`.

```js
hello();                 // works
function hello() { console.log("hi"); }

// bye();                // TypeError: bye is not a function
var bye = function () {};
```

Primitives are immutable and copied by value; comparing compares values. Objects (arrays and functions included) are copied by reference: the variable holds a pointer, so two variables can share and mutate the same object, and `===` compares identity, not content.

Why the others are wrong: strings are compared by value, so `"ab" === "a" + "b"` is `true`; assigning an object copies the reference, not a clone, so both variables see mutations; and `===` on objects checks identity, so `({ x: 1 }) === ({ x: 1 })` is `false`.

```js
const a = { x: 1 };
const b = a;
b.x = 2;
a.x;                       // 2
({ x: 1 }) === ({ x: 1 }); // false
```

Spread (`{...o}`) and `Object.assign` copy one level; nested objects are still shared. For a deep copy use `structuredClone`: built in, and it handles dates, maps, sets and cycles (but not functions or class prototypes). `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x))` loses `undefined`, turns dates into strings, drops `Map` and `Set`, and throws on cycles.

Why the others are wrong: `structuredClone` throws a `DataCloneError` on functions and returns plain objects for class instances (methods are lost); `Object.assign` and spread copy one level only; a JSON round-trip of a JS number is exact (the number is already a double), and the round-trip has real correctness problems, not just speed.

```js
const o = { a: 1, inner: { b: 2 }, when: new Date() };
const shallow = { ...o };
const deep = structuredClone(o);
shallow.inner === o.inner; // true
deep.inner === o.inner;    // false
```

A closure is a function that keeps access to the variables of the scope where it was created, even after that scope has returned. Uses: private state, factories, callbacks that remember context, memoisation, partial application. The cost: captured variables aren't garbage-collected while the closure lives.

Why the others are wrong: a closure keeps live access to the variables (later changes are visible), not a copy, and it keeps them alive; an IIFE is one use of a function, not the definition of a closure; and closures work after the outer function has returned, which is exactly why the captured scope is retained.

```js
function counter() {
  let n = 0;
  return () => ++n;
}
const next = counter();
next(); next(); // 1, 2
```

All three set `this` explicitly. `call(thisArg, a, b)` invokes now with listed arguments; `apply(thisArg, [a, b])` invokes now with an array; `bind(thisArg, ...preset)` returns a new function with `this` (and optionally leading arguments) fixed.

Why the others are wrong: `call` takes listed arguments and `apply` an array (not the other way round), and `bind` never schedules anything; `call` and `apply` invoke immediately while `bind` returns a function; and `bind` leaves the original function untouched, returning a new bound one.

```js
function greet(g, p) { return `${g}, ${this.name}${p}`; }
const u = { name: "Ana" };
greet.call(u, "Hi", "!");
greet.apply(u, ["Hi", "!"]);
const hi = greet.bind(u, "Hi");
hi("?");
```

Arrows have no own `this`, `arguments`, `super` or `new.target`; they can't be used with `new` and have no `prototype`. Great for callbacks that need the outer `this`; wrong for object methods that rely on their own `this`, and for constructors.

Why the others are wrong: arrows are expressions assigned to variables, so they follow `const`/`let` rules, and they don't get `this` from the object; they have no own `this`, and `new` on an arrow throws a `TypeError`; and the missing `this`, `arguments` and `prototype` are real semantic differences beyond syntax.

```js
const o = {
  n: 1,
  later() { setTimeout(() => console.log(this.n), 0); }, // 1
  bad: () => this,                                       // not o
};
```

Every object has an internal `[[Prototype]]` link. Property lookup walks the chain until it finds the property or reaches `null`. Writing sets the property on the object itself (shadowing). `Object.getPrototypeOf(x)` reads the link; `Object.create(p)` makes an object with prototype `p`. Methods shared via the prototype are stored once, not per instance.

Why the others are wrong: the prototype is a live link, so adding a method to it later is visible on existing objects; a plain assignment creates an own property that shadows the inherited one; and lookup starts with own properties, then walks up the chain.

```js
const animal = { eats: true };
const dog = Object.create(animal);
dog.barks = true;
dog.eats;                              // true (from prototype)
Object.getPrototypeOf(dog) === animal; // true
```

They are syntax over prototypes. A class body defines the constructor function; methods go on `Class.prototype`; `extends` links prototypes and `super` calls the parent. Differences from old constructor functions: a TDZ before the declaration, always strict, must be called with `new`, and support for `#private` fields and `static` members.

Why the others are wrong: methods are stored once on `Class.prototype` and shared through the chain; calling a class without `new` throws a `TypeError`, one of several differences from constructor functions; and `typeof SomeClass` is `"function"`.

```js
class Animal {
  #sound;
  constructor(s) { this.#sound = s; }
  speak() { return this.#sound; }
}
class Dog extends Animal { constructor() { super("woof"); } }
new Dog().speak(); // "woof"
typeof Animal;     // "function"
```

A higher-order function takes or returns functions (`map`, `filter`, `setTimeout`, decorators). Currying turns `f(a, b, c)` into `f(a)(b)(c)`, enabling partial application and reuse.

Why the others are wrong: a function that calls itself is recursive, and caching results is memoisation; chaining outputs into inputs is function composition, not currying; and binding `this` is `bind`, unrelated to higher-order functions.

```js
const curry = (fn) =>
  function curried(...args) {
    return args.length >= fn.length
      ? fn(...args)
      : (...more) => curried(...args, ...more);
  };
const add = curry((a, b, c) => a + b + c);
add(1)(2)(3); // 6
add(1, 2)(3); // 6
```

Memoisation caches a pure function's results by its arguments, trading memory for speed. It needs a stable cache key (serialise the arguments) and only suits pure functions. Watch for unbounded cache growth; consider an LRU, or a `WeakMap` for object keys.

Why the others are wrong: engines don't memoise your functions for you; caching compiled code is what the JIT does, not memoisation; and impure functions (API calls, time, randomness) give stale results when cached, while an unbounded cache is a memory leak.

```js
const memo = (fn) => {
  const cache = new Map();
  return (...args) => {
    const k = JSON.stringify(args);
    if (!cache.has(k)) cache.set(k, fn(...args));
    return cache.get(k);
  };
};
const fib = memo((n) => (n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)));
fib(50); // 12586269025
```

questions.prompt:
In what order does this log, and why?

```js
console.log("A");
setTimeout(() => console.log("timeout"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("promise"));
console.log("B");
```

questions.explanation:
JavaScript runs on one thread with one call stack. Async work (timers, I/O, network) is handled by the host (browser or Node), which queues callbacks. When the stack is empty the loop takes the next task. After each task it drains the whole microtask queue (promise reactions, `queueMicrotask`) before the next task or render. A long synchronous task blocks everything, including rendering.

Why the others are wrong: `.then` callbacks are queued as microtasks even for a resolved promise, so they never run in the middle of synchronous code; the microtask queue is drained before the next task, so the promise beats the timer; and no callback runs until the current synchronous code has finished.

```js
console.log("A");
setTimeout(() => console.log("timeout"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("promise"));
console.log("B");
// A, B, promise, timeout
```

Macrotasks (tasks): `setTimeout`, `setInterval`, I/O, UI events, `MessageChannel`. Microtasks: promise `.then/.catch/.finally`, `await` continuations, `queueMicrotask`, `MutationObserver`. The microtask queue is fully drained after the current task, so a promise chain always runs before the next timer, and an endless microtask loop starves rendering.

Why the others are wrong: the roles are swapped, timers and I/O are tasks and promise reactions are microtasks; the whole microtask queue is drained (including microtasks queued meanwhile), not one per task; and microtasks run on the same main thread, so an endless microtask loop starves rendering.

```js
setTimeout(() => console.log("macro"), 0);
queueMicrotask(() => console.log("micro 1"));
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("micro 2"));
// micro 1, micro 2, macro
```

`all`: waits for all, rejects on the first rejection (fail fast). `allSettled`: waits for all and reports each outcome; never rejects. `race`: settles with the first settled promise (good for timeouts). `any`: resolves with the first fulfilled one; rejects with an `AggregateError` only if all fail (good for redundant sources).

Why the others are wrong: `all` rejects on the first rejection and `allSettled` never rejects; `race` settles with the first settled promise, rejection included, while `any` waits for the first fulfilment and rejects only if all reject; and none of them run anything: the promises are already running when passed in.

```js
const results = await Promise.allSettled([a(), b(), c()]);
const ok = results.filter((r) => r.status === "fulfilled").map((r) => r.value);

const withTimeout = (p, ms) =>
  Promise.race([p, new Promise((_, rej) => setTimeout(() => rej(new Error("timeout")), ms))]);
```

Use `try/catch` around awaited calls; an `async` function always returns a promise, and a thrown error rejects it. Catch where you can act (retry, fallback, translate) and let the rest bubble; don't swallow errors. `fetch` only rejects on network failure, not on a 404 or 500, so check `res.ok`. A rejected promise that is never awaited or caught becomes an unhandled rejection.

Why the others are wrong: a single top-level catch can't retry or add context, and `fetch` resolves normally for 4xx/5xx responses; `.catch` works but `fetch` still doesn't reject on HTTP status, so there is no `err.status`; and it's the reverse: network failures reject, HTTP error statuses resolve with `res.ok === false`.

```js
async function load(id) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/items/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`); // fetch does not reject on 404
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error(`load(${id}) failed`, { cause: err });
  }
}
```

Awaiting in sequence makes independent calls run one after another. Start them together and await them together. In loops, `await` inside `for...of` is sequential on purpose, while `forEach` with an async callback doesn't wait at all. Bound concurrency for big lists (batches or a pool) to avoid overloading a server.

Why the others are wrong: `await` suspends only the current async function, not the thread, and `forEach` does not await its callbacks; the engine never parallelises awaits for you, and `forEach` returns `undefined`; and `forEach` doesn't await at all, so it can't even observe the errors (they become unhandled rejections).

```js
// slow: total = a + b
const x = await getA();
const y = await getB();

// fast: total = max(a, b)
const [x2, y2] = await Promise.all([getA(), getB()]);

// wrong: forEach does not await
items.forEach(async (i) => { await save(i); });
// right
await Promise.all(items.map(save));
```

A rejection with no handler attached by the time the microtask queue drains. Browsers emit `unhandledrejection`; modern Node terminates the process by default (since v15). Always return, await or catch promises; add a global handler only to log and shut down cleanly, not to carry on as if nothing happened.

Why the others are wrong: warning and continuing was the behaviour before Node 15, now the default mode is `throw`; a never-settling promise is not a rejection and Node doesn't time it out; and a rejection is handled as soon as any caller awaits or catches it, it needn't be caught where it was thrown.

```js
process.on("unhandledRejection", (reason) => {
  console.error("unhandled", reason);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

Promises can't be cancelled themselves; use `AbortController`. Pass its `signal` to `fetch` (and many Node APIs), call `abort()` to cancel, and handle the `AbortError`. Also use it for timeouts (`AbortSignal.timeout(ms)`) and to ignore stale responses in UI code.

Why the others are wrong: native promises have no `cancel()`, and `Promise.race` only stops waiting, the request keeps running; dropping the reference doesn't abort anything (the pending request keeps it alive); and a throw in `.then` rejects the derived promise, it doesn't travel back to the request.

```js
const ctrl = new AbortController();
const p = fetch("/api/slow", { signal: ctrl.signal });
setTimeout(() => ctrl.abort(), 500);
try { await p; } catch (e) { if (e.name !== "AbortError") throw e; }

await fetch("/api/x", { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(3000) });
```

Destructuring unpacks arrays and objects into variables, with defaults and renaming. Spread (`...x`) expands an iterable or object in place; rest (`...x`) collects the remaining items. Traps: spread copies are shallow, and defaults apply only for `undefined`, not `null`.

Why the others are wrong: spread copies one level (nested objects are shared), and defaults kick in for `undefined`, not for `0` or `""`; `const { d = 5 } = { d: null }` gives `d === null`; and a rest element must be last, while spread copies `undefined` values like any other.

```js
const { id, name: title = "n/a", ...others } = { id: 1, extra: true };
const [first, , third = 0, ...tail] = [1, 2, undefined, 4, 5];
const merged = { ...{ a: 1 }, ...{ a: 2, b: 3 } }; // { a: 2, b: 3 }
const sum = (...nums) => nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
```

CommonJS (`require`, `module.exports`) is synchronous, dynamic and Node's legacy format. ES modules (`import`/`export`) are static (enabling tree shaking), asynchronous, always strict, export live bindings and support top-level `await`. In Node, enable ESM with `.mjs` or `"type": "module"`. Use dynamic `import()` for lazy loading and to import ESM from CommonJS.

Why the others are wrong: top-level `await` is ESM-only and CommonJS is sloppy unless you add `"use strict"`; the bindings are reversed, ESM exports live bindings while `require` returns a snapshot object, and static imports are what makes tree shaking possible; and ESM modules are evaluated eagerly in dependency order, while `require` is synchronous.

```js
// math.mjs
export const add = (a, b) => a + b;
export default function mul(a, b) { return a * b; }
// app.mjs
import mul, { add } from "./math.mjs";
const lazy = await import("./big.mjs");
```

An iterable has `[Symbol.iterator]()` returning an iterator whose `next()` yields `{ value, done }`; `for...of`, spread and destructuring use it. A generator (`function*`) is a pausable function that `yield`s values lazily, handy for infinite sequences, custom iteration and cooperative flows. Async generators with `for await` stream async data.

Why the others are wrong: `for...of` uses `[Symbol.iterator]`, not `length` (array-likes without it throw), and generators are lazy; the iterator protocol returns an iterator object, not an array, and generators run on the calling thread; and `next()` returns `{ value, done }` objects, while promise-per-value is what async generators (`async function*`) do.

```js
function* ids() { let i = 1; while (true) yield i++; }
const it = ids();
it.next().value; // 1
it.next().value; // 2

async function* pages() { for (let p = 1; p <= 3; p++) yield await fetchPage(p); }
for await (const page of pages()) { /* ... */ }
```

A `Proxy` wraps an object and intercepts operations (get, set, has, deleteProperty, apply) through handler traps. `Reflect` offers the default behaviour for each operation, so traps can delegate. Uses: validation, observable state (Vue 3 reactivity), logging, default values. Costs: slower than plain access, and subtle invariants.

Why the others are wrong: a proxy wraps the original target without copying it, and `Reflect` has no equality check; proxies are slower than plain objects and there is no API to unwrap one; and handlers can trap `set`, `apply`, `construct`, `deleteProperty` and more, while `Reflect` mirrors those operations rather than reporting metadata.

```js
const guarded = new Proxy({}, {
  set(target, key, value, receiver) {
    if (key === "age" && !Number.isInteger(value)) throw new TypeError("age must be int");
    return Reflect.set(target, key, value, receiver);
  },
});
guarded.age = 30;
```

`const` protects the binding only. `Object.freeze` makes own properties read-only (shallow; it fails silently in sloppy mode and throws a `TypeError` in strict mode). Nested objects need recursive freezing or immutable update patterns (spread, `structuredClone`, Immer). `Object.defineProperty` gives per-property control (`writable`, `enumerable`, `configurable`).

Why the others are wrong: `const` affects the variable and `Object.freeze` affects the object, they're unrelated features; `Object.freeze` is shallow (nested objects stay mutable) and `const` doesn't touch properties at all; and a `const` object's properties can still be changed.

```js
"use strict";
const cfg = Object.freeze({ a: 1, nested: { b: 2 } });
// cfg.a = 2;        // TypeError
cfg.nested.b = 3;    // allowed (shallow freeze)
```

The JS code runs on one thread, but I/O is delegated to the OS (epoll/kqueue/IOCP) and to a libuv thread pool (file system, DNS, crypto, zlib), and results come back as callbacks into the event loop. CPU-bound work blocks the loop, so offload it to `worker_threads`, child processes or a separate service; scale across cores with the `cluster` module or multiple processes.

Why the others are wrong: the thread pool runs native work (file system, DNS lookup, crypto, zlib), never your JS callbacks; Node doesn't use a thread per request, so a CPU-heavy handler stalls every request; and marking a function `async` doesn't move it anywhere, its synchronous parts still block the event loop.

```js
import { Worker, isMainThread, parentPort, workerData } from "node:worker_threads";
if (isMainThread) {
  new Worker(new URL(import.meta.url), { workerData: 40 })
    .on("message", (r) => console.log("fib", r));
} else {
  const fib = (n) => (n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2));
  parentPort.postMessage(fib(workerData));
}
```

Debounce waits until calls stop for `ms`, then runs once (search-as-you-type, end of resize). Throttle runs at most once per `ms` while calls continue (scroll, mouse move). Both use closures and timers.

Why the others are wrong: they behave differently (debounce resets on every call, throttle rate-limits), regardless of which timer API implements them; debounce runs after a quiet period, not on the first call only, and throttle drops extra calls rather than queueing them; and the definitions are swapped.

```js
const debounce = (fn, ms) => {
  let t;
  return (...a) => { clearTimeout(t); t = setTimeout(() => fn(...a), ms); };
};
const throttle = (fn, ms) => {
  let last = 0;
  return (...a) => {
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now - last >= ms) { last = now; fn(...a); }
  };
};
```

An event travels down from the root to the target (capture), fires on the target, then bubbles back up. Handlers run on bubble by default. Delegation attaches one listener on a parent and uses `event.target` (or `closest`) to handle many children, including ones added later. `stopPropagation` halts the travel; `preventDefault` cancels the browser default; they're different things.

Why the others are wrong: capture goes down and bubbling goes up, and `stopPropagation` stops the travel rather than delegating; capturing is a phase of the same event (opt in with `{ capture: true }`), and delegation uses one listener on the parent; and listeners run in the bubble phase by default, while `once` only removes a listener after one call.

```js
list.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
  const li = e.target.closest("li");
  if (li && list.contains(li)) console.log("clicked", li.dataset.id);
});
```

The same-origin policy blocks a page from reading responses from another origin. CORS lets the server opt in with headers (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, `-Methods`, `-Headers`, `-Credentials`). "Non-simple" requests (custom headers, a JSON content type, `PUT`/`DELETE`) trigger a preflight `OPTIONS` request first. CORS is enforced by the browser only, protects users, and isn't an authentication mechanism. A wildcard `*` origin is rejected by browsers for credentialed requests, and reflecting any request's `Origin` back with `Allow-Credentials: true` is just as unsafe.

Why the others are wrong: CORS is enforced by the browser, not a server firewall, and simple requests (plain GET, form-style POST) have no preflight; it's the reverse, `PUT` and `DELETE` are non-simple and preflighted; and the preflight never carries cookies or credentials, CORS is not authentication.

```js
// Express-style
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.set("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", "https://app.example.com");
  res.set("Access-Control-Allow-Headers", "Content-Type, Authorization");
  if (req.method === "OPTIONS") return res.sendStatus(204);
  next();
});
```

`localStorage`: synchronous string key/values, persistent, per origin, about 5 MB, readable by any script (don't keep tokens there if XSS is a concern). `sessionStorage`: the same, but per tab session. Cookies: sent with every request, size-limited, can be `HttpOnly` (invisible to JS), so best for session ids. IndexedDB: asynchronous, structured, for large data and offline apps.

Why the others are wrong: any script on the origin (including injected XSS) can read `localStorage`, while `HttpOnly` cookies are hidden from JS; `sessionStorage` is per tab, cookies are tiny and sent with every request, and IndexedDB is asynchronous; and Web Storage is never sent to the server, cookies are limited to about 4 KB.

```js
localStorage.setItem("theme", "dark");
JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("prefs") ?? "{}");
```

The main thread runs JS, style, layout and paint, so long tasks (over 50 ms) cause jank. Move heavy computation to Web Workers, split work into chunks (`requestIdleCallback`, `scheduler.yield`), do visual updates in `requestAnimationFrame`, batch DOM reads before writes to avoid layout thrashing, and prefer CSS transforms for animation.

Why the others are wrong: timers run their callbacks on the main thread, so the loop still blocks rendering; `async` doesn't move code off the main thread; and interleaving reads and writes forces repeated synchronous layout (layout thrashing), while `setTimeout` isn't synced to the display like `requestAnimationFrame`.

```js
const w = new Worker("worker.js");
w.postMessage(bigArray);
w.onmessage = (e) => render(e.data);

function tick() { el.style.transform = `translateX(${x++}px)`; requestAnimationFrame(tick); }
requestAnimationFrame(tick);
```

Strict mode turns silent errors into exceptions (undeclared variables, duplicate parameters, assignments to read-only properties), sets `this` to `undefined` in plain calls, and is automatic in modules and classes. TypeScript adds static types, catching shape and null errors at build time and improving refactoring and editor support; it erases to plain JavaScript, so runtime rules still apply and external data still needs validation (for example with Zod).

Why the others are wrong: strict mode has no type checks and TypeScript types are erased at build time; strict mode doesn't freeze objects, and TypeScript can't check data at runtime (use a validator such as Zod); and the compiled JavaScript contains no type checks at all.

```js
"use strict";
// undeclaredVar = 1;   // ReferenceError
```

It tests promises, ordering and edge cases: preserve input order (not completion order), accept non-promise values (wrap them with `Promise.resolve`), resolve `[]` for empty input, reject on the first rejection, and count completions rather than relying on array length.

Why the others are wrong: `Promise.all` accepts plain values (wrapping them with `Promise.resolve`) and resolves `[]` for empty input; results follow input order and the first rejection settles it, the behaviour described is closer to `allSettled`; and a sequential `await` loop only notices a rejection when it reaches that item, so a fast failure behind a slow promise is reported late (and briefly unhandled).

```js
function promiseAll(items) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const list = [...items];
    const out = new Array(list.length);
    let left = list.length;
    if (left === 0) return resolve(out);
    list.forEach((item, i) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then((v) => {
        out[i] = v;
        if (--left === 0) resolve(out);
      }, reject);
    });
  });
}
await promiseAll([1, Promise.resolve(2), new Promise((r) => setTimeout(() => r(3), 10))]); // [1,2,3]
```
